WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
This is Campus Access: a white-label
engineering prototype

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.000
built around a typed Moroccan
number-plate recognition pipeline.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.500
The starting model could read
a still image.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.500
The product question was larger:

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.500
how should several gates, invitations,
incidents, and uncertain observations

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.000
become one safe operating workflow?

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.500
The command view prioritizes what
needs attention.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.500
Operators can see queue pressure, gate
state, recent arrivals, and degraded

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:35.000
equipment without treating missing data
as a successful decision.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.500
Inside a gate workspace, recognition
is evidence—not authority.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.500
Plate candidates, confidence, camera
health, and an access match

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:51.000
are shown together, while authorization
remains a separate, traceable action.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.500
Hosts submit a typed request with
a site and time window.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.500
An authorized reviewer can approve
or reject it with context,

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:07.000
creating a bounded grant instead of
passing free-form messages between teams.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.500
Operations brings incidents and device
heartbeat into the same model.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.000
A possible tailgating signal
and an intermittent camera

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.500
stay assigned, time-stamped,
and linked to follow-up

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.000
rather than disappearing
after the queue moves.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.500
The interface is white-label and supports
English, French, and Arabic

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.000
with right-to-left layout.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.500
Branding, locale, time zone, and API
location are configuration—not

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.000
campus-specific business logic.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Behind the interface, a typed FastAPI
control plane persists tenant-scoped

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.500
workflows. The target architecture
keeps model workers separate

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.000
and places an outbound edge agent
beside ONVIF and RTSP cameras.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.500
The result is deliberately honest:

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.000
deterministic synthetic data today,
explicit failure and recovery paths,

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.500
and a staged shadow pilot
before automation.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.000
It demonstrates product judgment
as well as working code.
